  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>On Saturday May 23, President Trump posted on Truth Social that "An Agreement has been largely negotiated, subject to finalization between the United States of America, the Islamic Republic of Iran, and the various other Countries," adding that "Final aspects and details of the Deal are currently being discussed, and will be announced shortly." The announcement followed calls with leaders from Qatar, Saudi Arabia, UAE, Pakistan, Jordan, Egypt, Turkey, and Bahrain, plus a separate call with Prime Minister Netanyahu. By Sunday May 24, Axios reported that a 60-day MOU draft had been approved at negotiator level — VP Vance, Witkoff, Kushner on the US side; Parliament Speaker Ghalibaf on the Iranian side — with terms including Hormuz opening without tolls, mine clearance by Iran, US port-blockade lift, and Iran's commitment to never pursue nuclear weapons followed by enrichment-suspension negotiations. The Washington Times, citing a source close to the talks, reported an expected Sunday-afternoon announcement (confirmed by multiple US outlets, CNN, PBS, NPR). → moved <span class="cite">F2</span>, <span class="cite">N1</span></p>

      <p>Iranian state media and officials offered a divided response. IRNA quoted a spokesperson as saying "the trend has been toward narrowing differences over the past week," the closest Tehran has come to publicly acknowledging progress. However, Times of Israel's May 23 liveblog reported that Iranian media contradicted the Hormuz terms, stating the Strait "will stay under Iran's management." Fars News — associated with the IRGC — characterized US media reporting as "propaganda justifying Trump's retreat." Trump told Axios in a separate interview that it was "50/50" whether to make a "good" deal or "blow them to kingdom come," adding he would make a decision by Sunday. No signed document has been confirmed by both Reuters and an Iranian principal-level source as of 09:00 Stockholm. → supports <span class="cite">F2</span> without move</p>

      <p>The Israeli Knesset passed a preliminary reading of the government's own dissolution bill by a vote of 110 to 0 on Wednesday May 20, with Netanyahu absent from the chamber. The bill — submitted by the coalition itself, not by the opposition — requires three additional readings before becoming law and does not name an election date, delegating that to the Knesset House Committee to set at least 90 days after final passage. Polling published May 19-20 by Times of Israel showed the pro-Netanyahu bloc at 49-51 seats — 10 short of the 61 needed to govern. Netanyahu's documented counter-move is reviving the Haredi draft-exemption bill as a side-deal to keep Degel HaTorah in the coalition. At no point between May 19-24 did Netanyahu make a formal public statement naming an election date. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span>, <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>The Lebanon 45-day ceasefire (extended May 15) reached its first horizon checkpoint at EOD May 22 without formal collapse. IDF struck more than 30 targets in southern Lebanon (Harouf, Borj El Chmali, Debaal) and ordered evacuations — but all strikes fell in south Lebanon outside the Beirut governorate. Hezbollah claimed responsibility for approximately 10 retaliatory attacks on Israeli troops in Lebanon and northern Israel. Neither the US State Department nor the Lebanese Foreign Ministry issued any statement withdrawing sponsorship or declaring the truce void. Pentagon military coordination talks remain scheduled for May 29 and political talks for June 2-3 in Washington. The Lebanon ceasefire forecast (F-Lebanon) is resolved RESOLVE-NO at its May 22 horizon date. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>AIS data and IRGC-announced transit figures for the Strait of Hormuz remained far below the 50-vessel/day threshold required for F1 resolution. Windward AI reported commercial shipping around Hormuz is "increasingly going dark," with multiple vessels switching off transponders. The LMA stated as of May 6 that safety concerns — not insurance availability — are the primary constraint on Hormuz transit, with war-risk premiums at 8x pre-war levels. MSC's bypass service from Antwerp launched May 10 with no announced return to the Strait. One Maersk vessel transited under US escort on May 4 as part of Project Freedom, which was paused May 6 amid diplomatic progress. Physical mine-clearing and shipping-line scheduling decisions have not been observed. → supports <span class="cite">F1</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

